// Title: Audit Policy Tampering Via NT Resource Kit Auditpol
// Sigma ID: c6c56ada-612b-42d1-9a29-adad3c5c2c1e
// Status: test | Level: high
// Author: Nasreddine Bencherchali (Nextron Systems)
// Date: 2021-12-18 | Modified: 2023-02-21
// ATT&CK: defense-impairment, T1685.001
// Description: Threat actors can use an older version of the auditpol binary available inside the NT resource
//   kit to change audit policy configuration to impair detection capability. This can be carried out
//   by selectively disabling/removing certain audit policies as well as restoring a custom policy
//   owned by the threat actor.
// False positives:
//   - The old auditpol utility isn't available by default on recent versions of Windows as it was replaced by a newer version. The FP rate should be very low except for tools that use a similar flag structure
// References:
//   - https://github.com/3CORESec/MAL-CL/tree/master/Descriptors/Windows%202000%20Resource%20Kit%20Tools/AuditPol
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/adf5a626c4a776f3f023056f0f398b9523a2f09e/rules/windows/process_creation/proc_creation_win_auditpol_nt_resource_kit_usage.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language 12.4.1, live Defender schema)
// Machine-generated and not tested against real telemetry. Verify before use as a detection.

DeviceProcessEvents
| where ProcessCommandLine contains "/logon:none" or ProcessCommandLine contains "/system:none" or ProcessCommandLine contains "/sam:none" or ProcessCommandLine contains "/privilege:none" or ProcessCommandLine contains "/object:none" or ProcessCommandLine contains "/process:none" or ProcessCommandLine contains "/policy:none"
